# Revisions to skip when running `git blame`.
#
# Only whole-tree mechanical reformats belong here — commits that changed how the code is written without
# changing what it does. Listing one keeps `git blame` pointing at the commit that last altered a line's
# meaning instead of the one that re-wrapped it.
#
# Enable it locally with:
#
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# GitHub applies this file automatically in its blame view.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ACTION REQUIRED AFTER MERGING #178
#
# This repo squash-merges, so the branch commit that carried the ruff reformat does not survive onto `main` —
# its SHA is rewritten into the single squash commit. A branch SHA listed here would therefore name an object
# that `main` does not contain, and git skips an unresolvable entry **silently**, so the file would look
# correct while doing nothing.
#
# The reformat is part of #178's squash commit. Once it is merged, append that commit's full 40-character SHA
# below:
#
#     git log --oneline -1 main        # the squash commit for #178
#
# and add it as:
#
#     # chore(lint,types): enforce the formatter and make mypy pass (#178)
#     <40-char sha>
#
# One full SHA per line, newest last, each with a comment naming what the commit did.
